ition as well  as microbial function  we  also hypothesized we would see a general  trend where the rhizo plane and the rise  of sphere would be different from each  other at both of the different root  orders so absorptive and our transporter  fine roots  and  we expected that because of the sort of  metabolic flux that goes on in the  absorbed defined rooms we thought we'd  see a lot more like simple sugars things  that are very easy to use  whereas we'd see maybe some more  defensive based compounds in the  transport of fine roots because it's  trying to prevent things from you know  trying to get in and like you know be  pathogenic  and so  the first pattern  what we're comparing here  is i'm comparing  the rhizoplane to the rhizosphere in  each specific root order  and so what we saw was that for the  absorptive fine roots we saw significant  differences between the rhizoplane and  the rhizosphere but we did not see that  for the riser uh for the transport of  fine roofs so the differences between  the root compartments were only seen for  the absorptive fine roots not  transported fine roots  so for this plot here this is just  another ordination  for the different shapes of the squares  here are the rhizo-plane and the  triangles of the rhizosphere  and the different colors are just  different tree species  and so i really like this graph because  it looks like a sort of a mirror copy of  each other because they're different  compartments so it's sort of like being  like pushed up a little bit and that's  just different degrees of host influence  there so we're seeing different microbes  what about the taxer  so  um  some of the taxes that appeared to  differ  between the rhizosphere and the  rhizoplane  one of them was the gamma proteobacteria  now with the recent changes in microbial  taxonomy which i can talk to you for  about for a week because no one was  really happy with it but the beta  proteobacteria that i was talking about  before the one that's ve