nt functional roles for the  riser plane but not the rhizosphere  and what about again the taxa who's  driving these patterns and again our  favorite one gamma proteobacteria which  is  beta proteobacteria really in this at  least in this study  we see way more of that in the  absorptive fine roots as opposed to the  transport defined roots for the  rhizoplane and again this mirrors the  pattern we saw in our first study  because absorb defined roots metabolic  hot spot we'd expect to see more of them  in that environment  for the root metabolome so the resources  available here this is again another  ordination um in this case the shapes  are different root orders we saw  significant differences in the root  metabolomes for the absorptive and  transported fine roots so the resources  available at these different fine roots  are different from each other  a lot of these differences were  primarily driven by sugars which is one  of our hypotheses so things like sucrose  tagatos down here fructose  things like that so the flocks between  those uh was driving a lot of the  differences between these different  route orders  and we also saw some defense compounds  such as catechin which is  i think it's that one there maybe  as being more of that in the transport  of fine groups so again our hypothesis  was on point where we were thinking  those more sort of defensive sort of  compounds in the transported fine routes  and lastly  i had talked about microbial functioning  you can get differences in composition  but the functions may not necessarily  differ from each other  luckily we did see differences in  functioning which is fantastic because  it's a lot of money to do metagenomics  and so for the absorptive fine roots  what we found was that we saw an  over-representation of functions  functions responsible for sugar  transport systems so seeing a lot more  sort of movement of sugar in that type  of environment which makes sense there's  a lot more sugars in that envi